# direnv enters the devenv automatically on cd. One-time per clone: `direnv allow`.
#
# A shell you have to remember to enter is a shell people work outside of, which is how
# "works on my machine" comes back. This makes the correct environment the default.
eval "$(devenv direnvrc)"
use devenv

# Reload when something that DEFINES the environment changes - notably the compiler pin,
# so a toolchain bump does not silently leave everyone on the old one.
watch_file devenv.nix
watch_file devenv.lock
watch_file rust-toolchain.toml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one hook for environment-specific settings, and it deliberately knows nothing about them.
#
# Some networks reach package registries, container registries and a Nix cache through a mirror
# or a proxy rather than directly. Those settings are a property of WHERE A MACHINE IS, not of
# this project, and this repository is public - so nothing that identifies a network belongs in
# it. What belongs here is the hook, and the hook is all this is.
#
# The file is OUTSIDE the repository on purpose, rather than a gitignored file inside it. An
# ignored file still leaks: `git add -f`, an archive, a tool that copies the tree, and it shows
# as untracked noise in `git status` forever. A path under the user's own config directory cannot
# end up in a commit here by accident, because it is not here.
#
# Absent, this is a no-op and everything resolves publicly - which is the correct default and
# the one CI runs with.
#
# What such a file may export - the contract, so a reader knows what to write without an example
# that would have to name something: the standard proxy variables and their lowercase twins; the
# Nix substituter settings; and `SUTURA_IMAGE_REGISTRY`, which every image in
# `compose.services.yaml` already reads. It should also PRINT one line saying what it did, because
# a slow public pull and a fast mirrored one look identical until you know which you got.
#
# `docs/enterprise-mirrors.md` describes the pattern generically.
local_env="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/sutura/env.sh"
watch_file "$local_env"
source_env_if_exists "$local_env"
